---
// src/pages/blog/bra-anatomy.astro
import BlogLayout from '../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';
import Picture from '../../components/Picture.astro';
import BlogSchema from '../../components/BlogSchema.astro';
import { blogSchemaBySlug } from '../../data/blog-schema';

const entry = blogSchemaBySlug['bra-anatomy'];
---
<BlogLayout
  title="Bra Anatomy: The 12 Components That Decide Cost and Fit"
  description="A factory-side breakdown of every component in a bra — cup, wire, wing, band, hook, slider — and how each one moves your unit cost and fit quality."
>
  <article class="container-site py-12 max-w-3xl">
    <nav class="breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span>
      <a href="/blog">Blog</a><span>/</span>
      <span class="text-navy font-medium">Bra Anatomy</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="aspect-[16/10] overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-cream-dark mb-8">
      <Picture src="blog-bra-anatomy-hero.webp" alt="Front view of an underwire lace bra showing cup, band and strap construction" cls="w-full h-full object-cover" priority sizes="(max-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 2rem), 704px" />
    </div>

    <time class="text-sm text-slate">November 8, 2026</time>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-navy mt-2 mb-8">Bra Anatomy: The 12 Components That Decide Cost and Fit</h1>

    <div class="prose prose-stone max-w-none">
      <p class="text-lg text-stone leading-relaxed">
        Most sourcing conversations about bras start with price. That is backwards. A bra is not one
        product — it is roughly twelve components assembled through a dozen sewing operations, and every
        single one of those components has a price floor and a fit consequence. When a quote comes in
        lower than everyone else, the difference almost never comes from labour efficiency. It comes
        from somebody quietly swapping a component.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the breakdown we walk buyers through on factory visits, written down so you can use it
        in your next supplier call.
      </p>

      <h2>1. The cup — four construction types, four price tiers</h2>
      <p>
        Cup construction is the single biggest cost driver in a bra, and the differences are not
        cosmetic. In ascending order of cost:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Unmoulded cut-and-sew.</strong> Fabric pieces cut to shape and stitched together. Cheapest to tool, most flexible for lace, but the hardest to get consistent — the fit is entirely in the pattern-making skill.</li>
        <li><strong>Moulded single-layer.</strong> Fabric heat-formed over a cup-shaped die. Consistent shape every time, but limited to simpler silhouettes.</li>
        <li><strong>Moulded double-layer (spacer).</strong> Two layers of spacer foam bonded. Gives opacity and support with breathability. Roughly 1.5–2× the cost of single-layer.</li>
        <li><strong>Contour / graduated push-up.</strong> Moulded cup with graduated thickness and often a gel or foam insert at the base. The highest tooling cost, and the one where cheap versions show themselves fastest — the padding collapses after a few dozen washes.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A buyer looking at two quotes that differ by 30% should first ask: which cup construction is
        each quoting? It is very often the entire answer.
      </p>

      <h2>2. The underwire</h2>
      <p>
        Wire is where a lot of hidden cost sits, because you cannot see it on a finished sample. Quality
        wire is nickel-free stainless, coated, with consistent gauge end to end. Budget wire is a thinner
        coated steel with variable gauge — it feels fine in the hand, then pokes through the channel after
        a season of wear. Ask specifically for the wire spec and whether it is nickel-free, because that
        is also a compliance question in the EU.
      </p>

      <h2>3. The wing</h2>
      <p>
        The wing is the side panel under the arm. In a wirefree bra it does the entire support job, so
        the fabric choice matters more than people expect. Two-way stretch power mesh gives more hold
        than a basic single jersey at a modest cost increase. If you have ever bought a wirefree bra that
        rolled up at the underarm within a month, that was a wing fabric decision.
      </p>

      <h2>4. The band and its elastic</h2>
      <p>
        Around 80% of a bra's support comes from the band, not the straps. Band elastic is graded by
        width and by how many cycles it survives before losing recovery — commonly described as
        "wash-cycle" grading. Cheaper elastic relaxes visibly after 20–30 home washes; better elastic
        holds past 60. On a B2B spec sheet this is worth pinning down in writing.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Straps and sliders</h2>
      <p>
        Three common constructions: rigid woven strap (cheapest, no give), plush-backed elastic
        (most comfortable), and fully adjustable elastic with metal or nylon hardware. Hardware is a
        quiet cost lever — nylon sliders cost a fraction of metal ones and are perfectly serviceable,
        but they read as cheaper to a customer handling the product.
      </p>

      <h2>6. The hook-and-eye closure</h2>
      <p>
        Standard is 2-hook or 3-hook. The cost difference is small; the fit difference is not. A 3-hook
        closure on a full-bust size distributes band tension noticeably better. Specifying 2-hook across
        an entire size range to save cost is a common mistake that shows up in your return rate rather
        than your invoice.
      </p>

      <h2>7. The ring and apex detail</h2>
      <p>
        Where the strap meets the cup. Plastic rings, metal rings, or a stitched fabric loop. This is a
        pure cost component with no fit consequence — a legitimate place to save money if your price
        point needs it.
      </p>

      <h2>8. The channel</h2>
      <p>
        The fabric tube the wire sits in. Cheap channel is a single-fold knit that the wire works its way
        out of. Better channel is a densely woven casing with reinforced ends. Again, invisible at
        sampling, obvious at month six.
      </p>

      <h2>9. The bridge and 10. the gore</h2>
      <p>
        The piece between the cups. On a plunge or low-gore style this is deliberately short, which is
        exactly why plunge bras are harder to grade — less bridge means less stability and the wire has
        to do more work. If you are developing a deep-plunge, budget extra pattern iterations.
      </p>

      <h2>11. Trim and 12. thread</h2>
      <p>
        Bows, charms, lace edges, and — the one everybody forgets — thread. Cheap thread snaps under the
        tension of a stretch seam. It is a rounding-error cost line that can create a genuine quality
        problem, and it is the first thing a cost-down exercise reaches for. Push back on it.
      </p>

      <figure class="my-8">
        <div class="aspect-[16/10] overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-cream-dark">
          <Picture src="blog-bra-anatomy-detail.webp" alt="Close-up of a lace bra cup showing seam lines and edge finishing" cls="w-full h-full object-cover" sizes="(max-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 2rem), 704px" />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="text-xs text-slate mt-2">Seam placement and edge finishing are where cut-and-sew bras are won or lost.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Where the cost actually sits</h2>
      <p>
        Across the styles we run, unit cost concentrates in a predictable order. Useful as a sanity check
        when a quote arrives and something feels off.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cup construction</strong> - the largest single lever. A moulded cup carries tooling and higher material cost; a cut-and-sew cup carries labour; bonded seamless carries machinery.</li>
        <li><strong>Fabric and lace</strong> - second. Lace with good stretch recovery and a consistent pattern repeat costs meaningfully more than a basic knit, and the repeat drives cutting waste.</li>
        <li><strong>Hardware</strong> - small per unit, but the hook-and-eye tape and the ring and slider quality decide how the bra feels after twenty washes.</li>
        <li><strong>Labour</strong> - proportional to panel count and to the number of separate operations the construction needs.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A wirefree bra with four panels often costs about the same as an underwire with two. You removed the
        wire, the casing and the insertion operation, and spent the saving on fabric and seams instead.
      </p>

      <h2>The components buyers forget to specify</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Hook-and-eye tape</strong> - column count of one, two or three changes band width and support. Leaving it unspecified means the factory picks, usually the cheapest.</li>
        <li><strong>Slider and ring finish</strong> - nylon-coated versus metal. Coated is lighter and cheaper; metal holds tension better on large sizes.</li>
        <li><strong>Strap width</strong> - should change with size. A 12 mm strap on a 40 band digs.</li>
        <li><strong>Elastic grade</strong> - specify elasticity and recovery separately, because they are different properties and cheap elastic can be good at one and bad at the other.</li>
        <li><strong>Side bone</strong> - on larger sizes, whether the wing carries a side bone changes support and comfort substantially.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How construction choices move the price</h2>
      <p>
        Three examples of the same silhouette, same size run, same colourway, priced very differently
        because of construction rather than material.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Two-panel cut-and-sew underwire</strong> - the reference point. Wire, casing, insertion operation, but minimal sewing and low material waste.</li>
        <li><strong>Four-panel wirefree</strong> - you remove the wire, the casing and the insertion operation, then add two extra panels, more seam allowance, more sewing minutes and firmer elastic. Unit cost lands close to the underwire version, often within five percent.</li>
        <li><strong>Bonded seamless</strong> - no sewing on the body, but bonding press time, higher tooling cost and per-lot peel testing. Cheapest at high volume, most expensive at low volume, and impossible to correct after the fact.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The pattern here is consistent: construction choice moves cost between labour, machinery and material,
        and rarely changes the total as much as buyers expect. What does change is the minimum quantity at
        which the construction makes sense.
      </p>

      <h2>How to use this in a supplier conversation</h2>
      <p>
        Do not ask "what is your price". Ask for a component-level bill of materials on one style and
        compare it line by line against a second supplier. In our experience, two factories quoting what
        sounds like the same bra will differ on cup type, wire spec, band elastic grade, or hook count —
        and nine times out of ten, once those four are aligned, the quotes converge to within 10% of each
        other.
      </p>
      <p>
        If they converge and one is still dramatically cheaper, that is not efficiency. That is a
        component you have not found yet.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 p-6 bg-cream border-l-4 border-accent">
      <p class="text-sm text-navy font-semibold">Need a component-level quote?</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-stone mt-1">We will send a line-by-line BOM for any style in our range — cup construction, wire spec, elastic grade, all of it. <a href="/contact" class="text-accent hover:underline">Request a BOM</a> or browse our <a href="/products/bras" class="text-accent hover:underline">bra range</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <BlogSchema slug="bra-anatomy" entry={entry} />

  </article>
</BlogLayout>
